# ripgrep-only overrides. Git does NOT read this file, so everything listed here
# stays gitignored and unpushable — this ONLY re-enables it for SEARCH.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# `company/` (.gitignore:15), `knowledge/` (:14) and `CLAUDE.md` (:23) are all
# excluded from the public repo — correctly, since company/ is the private
# business SSoT. But ripgrep honours .gitignore by default, so a search started
# at the repo root silently skipped every runbook, every requirement, every
# vendor file, every session note, AND the operating guide itself.
#
# Measured 2026-08-05:
#   rg -l "MaxAuthTries" .          -> No matches found      (FALSE NEGATIVE)
#   rg -l "MaxAuthTries" company/   -> operations.md, oracle.md
#
# A root-level "no matches" was an ABSENCE OF SEARCH, not an absence of the fact.
# That is how a world-open 3389/tcp rule on the Oracle box survived four separate
# "only 22/80/443" claims in this corpus: the two facts sat in different files, in
# different vocabularies, and no search ever brought them into contact.
#
# 🔴 RE-MEASURED 2026-08-14, AND THE EXAMPLE ABOVE IS NOW HALF FALSE — WHICH IS
#    ITSELF THE POINT, SO IT IS CORRECTED RATHER THAN DELETED.
#      rg -l "MaxAuthTries" company/  -> runbooks/operations.md, PROJECT_STATE.md
#    `oracle.md` IS NO LONGER IN company/. [ADR 054] moved the vendor set to the
#    shared brain at ../../nikatru/, a SIBLING of this repo. So the very command
#    this file offers as the REMEDY now returns an incomplete answer for the very
#    fact it was written about.
#
# 🔴 AND `.ignore` CANNOT FIX IT — for the same structural reason the `.claude/`
#    negation below could not. A negation re-includes a path that some ignore rule
#    excluded; it cannot reach OUTSIDE the search root. `!nikatru/` here would
#    match nothing, because nikatru/ is not under this directory at all.
#
# ✅ SO THE FIX IS AGAIN AN INVOCATION, NOT A RULE — SEARCH BOTH ROOTS BY NAME:
#      rg "<pattern>" . ../../nikatru/
#    Anything that greps only this repo for a BUSINESS fact — a vendor, an account
#    id, the legal name, a compliance date — is asking the wrong tree and will get
#    a clean, confident, wrong "no matches". CLAUDE.md's "Repo layout" section
#    carries the one line that says where the brain is.
#
# SAFETY: git does not read .ignore. Re-verify after any change with
#   git check-ignore -v company knowledge CLAUDE.md .claude .claude/secrets.env
# which must still report all of them as ignored.
# 🔴 REPOINTED 2026-08-15, AND IT WAS INERT FOR HOURS BEFORE ANYONE NOTICED.
# `company/` and `knowledge/` moved under `Private/` that morning. These two
# negations then matched NOTHING, while `.gitignore` gained `Private/` — so the
# whole private corpus went invisible to search again, by the same mechanism this
# file was written to defeat, in the same session that wrote the move.
#   MEASURED before the fix:
#     rg -l "PROJECT_STATE" .            -> 0 files under Private/
#     rg --no-ignore --hidden … Private  -> 81 files actually contain it
# Every measurement taken from the repo root between the move and this fix read a
# smaller corpus than it thought — including the measurements OF the move.
# 📌 A negation is a pointer at a path, and it goes silently inert the moment that
# path moves. It cannot fail loudly, because "no matches" is a valid answer.
# ⚠️ BOTH CASES, AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT FAILED WITHOUT SAYING SO. `.gitignore`
# carries `Private/` AND `private/`; the rule that actually matched was the
# LOWERCASE one (`git check-ignore` reported `.gitignore:23:private/`). A single
# `!Private/` left the search at zero and looked exactly like a fix. Windows'
# filesystem is case-insensitive; ripgrep's glob matching is not.
# ⚠️ AND THE CHILDREN NEED THEIR OWN NEGATIONS — the second failed fix.
# `.gitignore:14` (`knowledge/`) and `:15` (`company/`) are UNANCHORED, so they
# match at ANY depth: re-including `Private/` still left `Private/company/` (deleted
# 2026-08-15) and `Private/knowledge/` (deleted 2026-08-15) excluded by the old rules.
# Two negations that each looked correct, and the search stayed at zero through
# both. Measured after each attempt, which is the only reason this is not still
# broken — "no matches" never announces itself as an absence of search.
# 🔴 DELETED 2026-08-18 — `!Private/` AND `!private/` STOOD ON THESE TWO LINES,
#    AND THIS ONE CANNOT BE FIXED HERE. The private corpus left this repo: it is
#    now the SIBLING directory `../Project_Cross_Platform_Apps_Private/`, not a
#    subdirectory. Both negations then named a path that does not exist under this
#    root, so they matched NOTHING — the `!nikatru/` case above, arriving a second
#    time by the same structural route. A negation re-includes a path that some
#    ignore rule excluded; it CANNOT reach OUTSIDE the search root, and no spelling
#    of it can. Not `!../…`, not the lowercase twin, not both children.
#
#   MEASURED 2026-08-18, on a FIXTURE reproducing the post-move layout — the move
#   had NOT happened yet, so this is measured on a copy of the shape, not on the
#   real tree; the real tree is unverified until the move lands:
#     rg -l "<pattern>" .                  -> No matches found   EXIT 1   (negations present)
#     rg -l "<pattern>" .                  -> No matches found   EXIT 1   (negations deleted)
#     rg -l "<pattern>" . ../<sibling>/    -> ../<sibling>/note.md   EXIT 0
#   The first two lines are the whole argument. The negations changed NOTHING, so
#   keeping them buys no reach and costs the next reader a false assurance — and
#   note the EXIT 1: "no matches" is a valid answer, so this failure never announces
#   itself. For comparison, the SAME search from the root reached 64 files under
#   `Private/` on 2026-08-18 while the tree was still inside the repo.
#
#   They are DELETED rather than left inert, on this file's own precedent for
#   `!.claude/scripts/` and `!Private/company` (deleted 2026-08-15): a negation that looks like it
#   re-enabled search is worse than none, because the next auditor reads the line
#   and trusts the sweep. A reader who finds `!Private/` here cannot tell that the
#   directory it names is no longer under this root at all.
#
# ✅ SO THE FIX IS AGAIN AN INVOCATION, NOT A RULE — SEARCH BOTH ROOTS BY NAME:
#      rg "<pattern>" . ../Nikatru_Android_Apps_Private/
# 🔴 THE LINE ABOVE WAS REPOINTED 2026-08-19 — IT READ `../Project_Cross_Platform_Apps_Private/`,
#    AND IT HAD GONE STALE IN THE ONE WAY THIS FILE CANNOT SURVIVE. That directory does not exist
#    under any root any more: the sibling was renamed with the other four on 2026-08-19. And per
#    the ⚠️ note below, `rg` over a MISSPELLED root fails LOUDLY (os error 2, EXIT 2) — so this one
#    would at least have shouted. That is the good case, and it is luck, not design: the same file
#    records that an EMPTY root exits 0 in silence. A remedy invocation carrying a dead path is the
#    same defect as the inert negations this file deleted — it looks like it re-enabled search.
#    ⚠️ AND THE DEPTH CHANGED TOO, WHICH `..` HAPPENS TO SURVIVE: this repo moved to
#    `Projects/Google_Play_Store/Android/Apps/`, and the corpus moved WITH it as its sibling. `..`
#    counts nothing — a sibling is a sibling at any depth — so only the NAME needed the edit. That
#    is the whole argument for addressing by relationship rather than by level count.
#    Anything that greps only this repo for a PRIVATE-corpus fact — PROJECT_STATE,
#    a runbook, a requirement, an ADR, a session note — is asking the wrong tree and
#    will get a clean, confident, wrong "no matches". That now makes THREE corpora
#    this file can no longer reach with a rule and must name on the command line:
#    `.claude/` (hidden, below), `../../nikatru/` (the business brain, above), and
#    this one. A search that names only `.` is reading a fraction of what it thinks.
#
# ⚠️ MID-MOVE WINDOW, MEASURED 2026-08-18 ON THE REAL TREES — BOTH EXIST AND THE
#    DESTINATION IS EMPTY. `../Project_Cross_Platform_Apps_Private/` has been
#    CREATED but holds ZERO files (`find … -type f` -> 0); the corpus is still in
#    `Private/` here. So the ✅ command above resolves, prints NO warning, and exits
#    **0** while returning nothing from the sibling — the same silent shape this
#    whole file is about, one layer up. Note the asymmetry, both measured:
#      rg … ./NOPE_DOES_NOT_EXIST/  -> "IO error … (os error 2)"   EXIT 2   (LOUD)
#      rg … ../<empty destination>/ -> no warning at all           EXIT 0   (SILENT)
#    A MISSPELLED root fails loudly; an EMPTY one does not. After the move, confirm
#    the sibling actually answers — `rg -l PROJECT_STATE ../Project_Cross_Platform_Apps_Private/`
#    must return files, not zero — before trusting any sweep that names it.
#    ✅ THE MOVE LANDED AND THE CHECK WAS RUN, 2026-08-19. The 2026-08-18 paragraph above is left
#    exactly as written — it is a dated measurement of the mid-move window and renumbering it
#    would falsify the record. Re-run under the CURRENT name, the sibling answers:
#      rg -l PROJECT_STATE ../Nikatru_Android_Apps_Private/   -> files, EXIT 0
#    So the "empty destination exits 0 in silence" hazard is discharged for this corpus.
#    UNTIL the move lands, the corpus is still here and reachable by NAMING IT, which
#    defeats `.gitignore` exactly as the `.claude/scripts/` case below does:
#      rg "<pattern>" . Private/        # measured 2026-08-18: 64 files, EXIT 0
#    Deleting the negations above cost that reach from a bare `rg …  .` on this day
#    and no other: they were about to go inert anyway, and an inert negation would
#    have hidden the same 64 files while looking like it published them.
#
# 📌 The privacy property did not weaken, it strengthened: the corpus is now OUTSIDE
#    the work tree, so the public repo has no path to it at all — the same argument
#    [ADR 054] made for moving `personal/ITR` out. `.gitignore`'s `Private/` and
#    `private/` lines stay as-is; they cost nothing and they still catch a stray
#    `Private/` recreated inside this repo.
#
# ⚠️ The `── FLATTENED 2026-08-15 ──` record immediately below narrates the tree as
#    it stood THEN, including what `!Private/` reached. It is left exactly as
#    written — renumbering or rewording a dated measurement falsifies the record
#    rather than repairing it. Read it as history, not as current reach.
# ── FLATTENED 2026-08-15 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `!Private/company/` and `!Private/knowledge/` (deleted 2026-08-15) stood here until
# the flatten merged both into one repo whose top level is topics. They are deleted rather
# than kept-and-inert: the note above records that the CHILDREN needed their own
# negations, and a reader who finds two negations for directories that no longer
# exist cannot tell whether they are load-bearing. Measured after deleting them —
# `rg -l PROJECT_STATE Private` reaches 69 files, the same 69 as before the move,
# because `!Private/` re-includes the whole tree in one line now that the tree is
# one directory. Fewer negations, same reach; verified, not assumed.
#
# 🔴 `Private.pre-flatten/` IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* NEGATED, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
# It is the frozen pre-migration copy kept until the restore gate passes, and it
# holds a COMPLETE SECOND COPY of every file below. Negating it would double every
# search hit in this corpus and silently offer a stale line beside the live one —
# and a wrong hit is indistinguishable from a right one in a search result. It
# stays invisible to `rg` (via `.gitignore`'s `private.*/`) for as long as it
# exists, and it is meant to stop existing.
!CLAUDE.md

# ── ADDED 2026-08-13, and DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN `.claude/` ──────────────
# The same false negative recurred one directory over. `.claude/` is gitignored
# (.gitignore:13) AND hidden, and ripgrep skips hidden dot-directories EVEN UNDER
# `--no-ignore` — so only `rg --hidden --no-ignore` ever reached it. An audit of
# "what still reads the old rclone remotes" therefore returned a clean FALSE
# NEGATIVE over the very directory holding the backup scripts:
#
#   rg -c --no-ignore "gcrypt:|gdrive:" .   -> 16 files, ZERO from .claude/
#   rg -c --hidden --no-ignore "…"          -> 29 files, incl. .claude/scripts/
#                                              backup-project.ps1 (7 hits)
#
# 🔴 THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `!.claude/…` LINE HERE, FOR TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS.
#
# 1. A NEGATION CANNOT WORK AT THIS PATH — TESTED, NOT ASSUMED. `.gitignore:13`
#    excludes the DIRECTORY `.claude/`, and neither git nor ripgrep can re-include
#    a path whose parent directory is excluded. `!.claude/scripts/` was written
#    here on 2026-08-13 and MEASURED INERT: a root `rg -l "gbiz:" .` still reached
#    zero files under .claude/, with and without `--hidden`. It was removed rather
#    than left in place, because a negation that looks like it re-enabled search
#    is worse than none — the next auditor reads the line and trusts the sweep.
#    Making it work needs `!.claude/` (the whole vault) plus a DENYLIST of secret
#    files, which fails OPEN for every secret file added later. Not acceptable.
#
# 2. WE DO NOT WANT IT ANYWAY. `.claude/` is the vault: `secrets.env`, `PEM.txt`,
#    `pack-signing.seed`, `Google drive_client_secret.json`, `nikatru-platform-*.json`
#    (a GCP service-account key), `rclone-box.conf`. Re-enabling it for search puts
#    all of them on the path of any routine `rg`, and an agent's search results land
#    in a transcript.
#
# ✅ SO THE FIX IS AN INVOCATION, NOT A RULE. When auditing "what still reads X",
#    .claude/ must be swept EXPLICITLY — an explicit path defeats both the ignore
#    rules and the hidden-directory skip:
#
#      rg "gcrypt:|gdrive:" .claude/scripts/     # explicit path — always searched
#      rg --hidden --no-ignore "…" .             # or sweep everything, knowingly
#
#    Measured 2026-08-13: `rg -c --no-ignore "gcrypt:|gdrive:" .` found 16 files and
#    ZERO from .claude/, while the explicit sweep found `backup-project.ps1` with 7
#    hits. A root-level "no matches" over a HIDDEN gitignored directory is an absence
#    of search, not an absence of the fact — the same lesson as company/ above, which
#    a negation could fix and this one cannot.
